1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:10,350
In recent years, researchers from diverse fields have agreed that short-termism is now a significant problem in industrialised societies.
近年来，不同领域的研究者一致认为，短视主义如今已成为工业化社会的一个重大问题。

2
00:00:11,150 --> 00:00:21,962
The inability to engage with longer-term causes and consequences leads to some of the world’s most serious problems: climate change, biodiversity collapse, and more.
无法关注更长远的原因和后果，导致了世界上一些最严重的问题：气候变化、生物多样性崩溃，等等。

3
00:00:22,762 --> 00:00:34,652
The historian Francis Cole argues that the West has entered a period where “only the present exists, a present characterised at once by the cruelty of the instant and by the boredom of an unending now”.
历史学家弗朗西斯·科尔认为，西方已进入这样一个时期：“只有当下存在，而这个当下的特征既是瞬间的残酷，又是无尽此刻的乏味”。

4
00:00:35,452 --> 00:00:46,982
It has been proved that people have a bias towards the present, focusing on loud attractions in the moment at the expense of the health, well-being and financial stability of their future selves or community.
已经证实，人们存在偏向当下的倾向：他们专注于眼前喧闹的诱惑，却以牺牲未来的自己或未来社群的健康、福祉和财务稳定为代价。

5
00:00:47,782 --> 00:00:51,482
In business, this bias surfaces as short-sighted decisions.
在商业中，这种偏向表现为短视的决策。

6
00:00:52,282 --> 00:01:01,072
And on slow-burning problems like climate change, it translates into the unwillingness to make small sacrifices today that could make a major difference tomorrow.
而在气候变化这类缓慢恶化的问题上，它就表现为人们不愿在今天做出小小的牺牲，而这些牺牲可能在明天带来重大改变。

7
00:01:01,872 --> 00:01:07,482
Instead, all that matters is next quarter’s profit, or satisfying some other near-term desires.
相反，人们只在乎下一季度的利润，或是满足其他某些眼前的欲望。

8
00:01:08,282 --> 00:01:11,922
These biased perspectives cannot be blamed on one single cause.
这些带有偏向的看法不能归咎于单一原因。

9
00:01:12,722 --> 00:01:17,002
It is fair to say, though, that our psychological biases play a major role.
不过，平心而论，我们的心理偏向起着重要作用。

10
00:01:17,802 --> 00:01:23,682
People’s hesitancy to delay satisfaction is the most obvious example, but there are others.
人们不愿延迟满足是最明显的例子，但例子不止这一个。

11
00:01:24,482 --> 00:01:30,432
One of them is about how the most accessible information in the present affects decisions about the future.
其中一种偏向涉及当下最容易获取的信息如何影响有关未来的决策。

12
00:01:31,232 --> 00:01:37,764
For instance, you might hear someone say: “It’s cold this winter, so I needn’t worry about global warming.”
比如，你可能会听到有人说：“今年冬天很冷，所以我不必担心全球变暖。”

13
00:01:38,564 --> 00:01:46,694
Another is that loud and urgent matters are given too much importance, making people ignore longer-term trends that arguably matter more.
另一种是人们过分看重喧闹而紧迫的事情，从而忽视了那些可以说更为重要的长期趋势。

14
00:01:47,494 --> 00:01:52,764
This is when a pop star draws far more attention than, say, gradual biodiversity decline.
这时就会出现这样的情况：一位流行歌星远比诸如生物多样性逐渐减少之类的问题更受关注。

15
00:01:53,564 --> 00:02:00,976
As a psychologist once joked, if aliens wanted to weaken humanity, they wouldn’t send ships; they would invent climate change.
一位心理学家曾开玩笑说，如果外星人想削弱人类，他们不会派飞船来，而是会发明气候变化。

16
00:02:01,776 --> 00:02:13,936
Indeed, when it comes to environmental transformations, we can develop a form of collective “poor memory”, and each new generation can believe the state of affairs they encounter is nothing out of the ordinary.
的确，说到环境变化，我们可能会形成某种集体性的“记性差”，而每一代新人都可能认为，自己所遇到的状况没有什么不寻常。

17
00:02:14,736 --> 00:02:21,696
Older people today, for example, can remember a time with insect-covered car windscreens after long drives.
比如，如今上了年纪的人能记得那样一个年代：长途驾车之后，汽车挡风玻璃上会布满昆虫。

18
00:02:22,496 --> 00:02:28,166
Children, on the other hand, have no idea that the insect population has dropped dramatically.
而孩子们则不知道，昆虫的数量已经急剧下降。
